            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the French for to lead to, then the French for to follow."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: puisque",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "puisque — \"since, as (a known reason)\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Puisque tu es là, tu peux m'aider. Since you are here, you can help me."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Takes the indicative: puisque tu es là. The reason is one both people already know. Before il or elle: puisqu'il."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "puisque is built from puis \"then, afterwards\" and que. puis is usually traced to a Vulgar Latin form of post \"after\" (Lewis & Short)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for telling, explaining and describing at B1."
